---
/**
 * /about — the page a sceptical reader opens before deciding whether to spend
 * thirty live hours with somebody, or to put an outside reviewer in front of a
 * design their team already argued about.
 *
 * THE ROADMAP ROW THIS PAGE IS WRITTEN AGAINST
 *
 *   "Reader checking who is teaching/advising. Sceptical of vague authority;
 *    connect verified experience to current approach. Evidence: source-backed
 *    roles and approved current work. Next step: programme or advisory."
 *
 * The whole difficulty of an About page is that the reader's scepticism is
 * correct, and the ordinary cure — more biography — makes it worse. So the
 * page spends most of its length on HOW A DESIGN QUESTION GETS ANSWERED,
 * because that is the thing a reader can actually evaluate, and it keeps the
 * biography to the single sentence that can be sourced.
 *
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * WHAT MUST NEVER BE ADDED HERE
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *
 *   * Job titles, levels, tenure, team sizes, user counts, revenue figures,
 *     named clients, employer endorsements, awards or press mentions. The
 *     verified claim is exactly: "engineering and leadership experience from
 *     Google, Amazon, Walmart and startups". Everything past that full stop is
 *     unsourced until Sunil confirms it, and an About page is the single
 *     easiest place on a website to launder an unchecked number into a fact.
 *   * A first-person workplace anecdote written on his behalf. The review
 *     notes on his own campaign posts say it twice — "illustrative scenario,
 *     no personal incident asserted". Inventing a story he then has to live
 *     with is worse than inventing a statistic, because he cannot correct it
 *     without contradicting his own page.
 *   * A quotation attributed to him. The VSL script is approved COPY, not
 *     approved SPEECH: its own review note says "Sunil to confirm natural
 *     phrasing". Nothing on this site puts words in quotation marks next to
 *     his name until he has said them.
 *
 * The worked example below is the site's house example — the returns assistant
 * from the VSL script and the cohort page — carried through in the same
 * neutral register the cohort page uses. Reusing it is deliberate: a reader
 * moving between pages should meet one system being examined from different
 * angles, not four unrelated illustrations.
 */
import PracticeLayout from '../layouts/PracticeLayout.astro';
import { COMMITMENT } from '../data/offer-display';

const title = 'About Sunil Mathew — The Living Craft';
const description =
  'Sunil Mathew teaches the design of agentic systems and advises engineering teams on those decisions. Engineering and leadership experience from Google, Amazon, Walmart and startups.';
---

<PracticeLayout title={title} description={description} canonical="/about/">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>About <em>Sunil Mathew</em></h1>
      <p class="credential">
        Sunil Mathew teaches the design of agentic systems and advises engineering teams
        making those decisions. He brings engineering and leadership experience from
        <strong>Google, Amazon, Walmart and startups</strong>. His focus is the reasoning
        behind a system’s design and the evidence that helps a team make its next
        decision.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/programmes/">See the programmes&nbsp;<span class="arrow">→</span></a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/advisory/">Advisory</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-note">
        This page states what can be sourced. Where a fact is outstanding it is named
        rather than filled in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── who this is for ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Who this page is for</p>
      <h2>You are deciding whether this <em>transfers</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose stack">
      <p>
        Two readers arrive here. One is weighing thirty live hours against everything
        else that could fill them. The other has a design decision in front of a team
        and is deciding whether an outside reviewer would help or merely add an
        opinion.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both are asking the same question, and it is not about credentials. It is
        whether the way this person reasons about a system transfers to the system you
        are responsible for. That question is answerable, so the rest of this page
        answers it rather than listing achievements.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── the worked example ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">An illustrative example</p>
      <h2>How a design question <em>gets answered</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose intro">
      <p>
        A returns assistant reads a policy, checks a request and recommends a refund.
        Someone asks whether it can issue the refund as well. On a diagram that is one
        more connection. In production the system can now move money.
      </p>
      <p>
        The example is illustrative and no customer incident is claimed. What follows
        is the order the questions get asked in — the same order the programme and an
        advisory review both use.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="tlist">
      <li>
        <span class="n">01</span>
        <div>
          <h4>Separate the recommendation from the action</h4>
          <p>
            Gathering policy evidence and preparing an answer is one responsibility.
            Moving money is another. They look like one box and they need different
            controls.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">02</span>
        <div>
          <h4>Ask what evidence permits the action</h4>
          <p>
            Not what the prompt says is permitted. What the tool itself will refuse to
            do when the evidence it was handed does not hold up.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">03</span>
        <div>
          <h4>Decide what happens when the answer is uncertain</h4>
          <p>
            A request outside the policy needs a clear next step. A tool that times out
            needs a way to examine the state of the action before repeating it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">04</span>
        <div>
          <h4>Ask what you can see afterwards</h4>
          <p>
            Which evidence was used, what the tool did, whether the workflow stayed
            inside its boundaries. Without that, evaluation cannot tell you which
            decision to change.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p class="aside">
      Each of those four is worked through in full in the
      <a class="textlink" href="/resources/">published guides</a>, which is the most
      direct evidence available of how the reasoning actually runs.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ── evidence ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">What is verified</p>
      <h2>The claims on this site, and <em>their source</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="panel facts">
      <div class="detail-row">
        <span class="dk">Experience</span>
        <span class="dv">Engineering and leadership at Google, Amazon, Walmart and startups</span>
      </div>
      <div class="detail-row">
        <span class="dk">Based</span>
        <span class="dv">Bengaluru, India</span>
      </div>
      <div class="detail-row">
        <span class="dk">Focus</span>
        <span class="dv">The reasoning behind a system’s design, and the evidence behind a team’s next decision</span>
      </div>
      <div class="detail-row">
        <span class="dk">Teaching</span>
        <span class="dv">{COMMITMENT} Live, with a working build</span>
      </div>
      <div class="detail-row">
        <span class="dk">Published method</span>
        <span class="dv"><a class="textlink" href="/resources/">Guides on agentic system design</a></span>
      </div>
      <div class="detail-row">
        <span class="dk">Elsewhere</span>
        <span class="dv">
          <a class="textlink" href="https://linkedin.com/in/sunil-mathew-466615a" rel="me">LinkedIn</a>
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside class="pending">
      <h3>Not stated on this page, and why</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Job titles, levels, tenure and team scope at each employer.
          <span class="who">Sunil to confirm what may be stated publicly</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          Current work — the products and engagements he is building now.
          <span class="who">Sunil to approve a description before it is published</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          Client names, project outcomes, learner counts and any numeric result.
          <span class="who">None are claimed; none will be added without a source</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </aside>
  </section>

  {/* ── next step ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Next step</p>
      <h2>Two ways to <em>work together</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cards2">
      <a class="card" href="/programmes/">
        <span class="k">Learning</span>
        <h3>Programmes</h3>
        <p>
          The open cohort for an individual, employer-funded places, and a group
          programme for a team. Build a working agentic system and practise explaining
          the decisions behind it.
        </p>
        <span class="go">Compare the routes <span class="arrow">→</span></span>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="/advisory/">
        <span class="k">Guidance</span>
        <h3>Advisory</h3>
        <p>
          Framing a use case, reviewing an architecture and agreeing the next useful
          piece of work. Deliberately bounded, and not a delivery engagement.
        </p>
        <span class="go">What it covers <span class="arrow">→</span></span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="aside">
      A specific question that fits neither?
      <a class="textlink" href="/contact/">Contact</a> lists which route goes where.
    </p>
    <p class="revised">
      Written <time datetime="2026-09-11">11 September 2026</time>. Pending Sunil’s
      factual approval.
    </p>
  </section>
</PracticeLayout>

<style>
  /* The hero's own lede is .credential, which global.css already sizes and
     colours for the noir shell. Everything below is page-local. */
  .hero .credential strong {
    color: var(--text-on-invert);
  }

  .intro {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .aside {
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    font-size: var(--size-3);
    color: var(--text-quiet);
    line-height: var(--lh-normal);
  }

  /* A white card holding the detail rows. .panel supplies the surface and the
     shadow; the rows supply their own hairlines, so the last one drops its
     border rather than doubling up against the card edge. */
  .facts .detail-row:last-child {
    border-bottom: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
  }
  .facts .detail-row:first-child {
    padding-top: 0;
  }
  .facts .dv {
    max-width: 46ch;
  }

  .pending {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  .revised {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--size-1);
    color: var(--text-quiet);
  }

  /* 390px: a label and its value stop sharing a line long before this, so the
     row becomes two stacked lines with the value left-aligned. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .facts .detail-row {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: var(--space-1);
    }
    .facts .dv {
      text-align: left;
    }
  }
</style>
